WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.852
This lesson is more than one formula.

2
00:00:04.852 --> 00:00:09.704
It is a whole system of linked meanings.

3
00:00:09.704 --> 00:00:14.556
Frequency tells you how many cycles or fronts are launched each second.

4
00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:19.409
Wavelength tells you how far apart matching points on the wave are, such as crest to crest or compression to compression.

5
00:00:19.409 --> 00:00:24.261
Wave speed tells you how quickly the disturbance moves through the medium.

6
00:00:24.261 --> 00:00:29.113
The key is that these are not three unrelated numbers.

7
00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:33.965
They are tied together by one relationship.

8
00:00:33.965 --> 00:00:38.817
If the source launches fronts more often, or if matching fronts are spaced farther apart, the wave can travel faster.

9
00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:43.669
The worked example will become much easier if those meanings are secure before any substitution begins.

10
00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:48.521
Keep this takeaway in view: Frequency, wavelength, and speed must be read as one linked wave system.

11
00:00:48.521 --> 00:00:53.374
Launch rate means frequency.

12
00:00:53.374 --> 00:00:58.226
How many wave cycles or fronts are produced each second.

13
00:00:58.226 --> 00:01:03.078
Front spacing means wavelength.

14
00:01:03.078 --> 00:01:07.930
The distance between matching points on neighboring cycles.

15
00:01:07.930 --> 00:01:12.997
This board is powerful because it shows the three quantities in their natural order.

16
00:01:12.997 --> 00:01:18.063
First, the source launches fronts at a certain rate.

17
00:01:18.063 --> 00:01:23.130
That is frequency.

18
00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:28.197
Second, the fronts are separated by a certain spacing.

19
00:01:28.197 --> 00:01:33.263
That is wavelength.

20
00:01:33.263 --> 00:01:38.330
Third, those two features together determine how quickly the pattern crosses the medium.

21
00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:43.397
That is wave speed.

22
00:01:43.397 --> 00:01:48.463
A student who only memorizes the letters often loses the meaning under exam pressure.

23
00:01:48.463 --> 00:01:53.530
A student who sees launch rate, front spacing, and travel speed as one system can rebuild the formula even if the symbols momentarily vanish.

24
00:01:53.530 --> 00:01:58.597
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula works best when every symbol is tied to a visible part of the wave picture.

25
00:01:58.597 --> 00:02:03.663
Frequency: Count how many cycles or fronts are launched each second.

26
00:02:03.663 --> 00:02:08.730
Wavelength: Measure the spacing between matching points such as crest to crest.

27
00:02:08.730 --> 00:02:13.426
Imagine a launch line sending identical carts onto a straight track.

28
00:02:13.426 --> 00:02:18.121
If the machine launches more carts each second, the track pattern updates more rapidly.

29
00:02:18.121 --> 00:02:22.817
If the machine also spaces the carts farther apart, the pattern stretches over more distance from one matching cart to the next.

30
00:02:22.817 --> 00:02:27.513
Wave reasoning is similar.

31
00:02:27.513 --> 00:02:32.209
Frequency is like the launch rate.

32
00:02:32.209 --> 00:02:36.904
Wavelength is like the spacing between repeated markers.

33
00:02:36.904 --> 00:02:41.600
Speed is the combined effect in how the whole pattern advances.

34
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:46.296
The analogy helps because it prevents students from treating frequency and wavelength as disconnected labels.

35
00:02:46.296 --> 00:02:50.991
They are both ingredients in the moving pattern.

36
00:02:50.991 --> 00:02:55.687
Keep this takeaway in view: Wave speed depends on two ingredients at once: launch rate and front spacing.

37
00:02:55.687 --> 00:03:00.383
Launch rate means more each second.

38
00:03:00.383 --> 00:03:05.079
That corresponds to a larger frequency.

39
00:03:05.079 --> 00:03:09.774
Spacing means bigger gaps.

40
00:03:09.774 --> 00:03:14.470
That corresponds to a larger wavelength.

41
00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:19.364
The equation is v equals f times lambda.

42
00:03:19.364 --> 00:03:24.258
In words, wave speed equals frequency multiplied by wavelength.

43
00:03:24.258 --> 00:03:29.151
But stop there and the lesson is not finished.

44
00:03:29.151 --> 00:03:34.045
Frequency is measured in hertz, which means per second.

45
00:03:34.045 --> 00:03:38.939
Wavelength is measured in metres.

46
00:03:38.939 --> 00:03:43.833
When those are multiplied, the result becomes metres per second, which matches wave speed.

47
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:48.726
Units are a hidden check on the reasoning.

48
00:03:48.726 --> 00:03:53.620
They also help you spot mistakes.

49
00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:58.514
If the wavelength is given in centimetres, convert it before substituting.

50
00:03:58.514 --> 00:04:03.408
If two of the three quantities are known, the equation can be rearranged to find the third, but only after the meanings and the units are secure.

51
00:04:03.408 --> 00:04:08.301
Keep this takeaway in view: The wave equation becomes dependable when meaning and units travel with the symbols.

52
00:04:08.301 --> 00:04:13.195
Meaning first: Say what each symbol means before you substitute numbers.

53
00:04:13.195 --> 00:04:18.089
Units check: Metres and seconds should line up with the final speed unit.

54
00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:22.982
v = f x lambda.

55
00:04:22.982 --> 00:04:27.876
frequency in hertz means cycles per second.

56
00:04:27.876 --> 00:04:32.770
Use the symbols only after you can say what each quantity means physically and what units it carries.

57
00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:38.514
A deeper pattern in this lesson is that the same speed can come from different pairs.

58
00:04:38.514 --> 00:04:44.257
A wave with frequency four hertz and wavelength three metres has speed twelve metres per second.

59
00:04:44.257 --> 00:04:50.001
Another wave with frequency six hertz and wavelength two metres also has speed twelve metres per second.

60
00:04:50.001 --> 00:04:55.745
The pairs are different, but the product is the same.

61
00:04:55.745 --> 00:05:01.488
This matters because it teaches students to reason with the relationship instead of grabbing one variable in isolation.

62
00:05:01.488 --> 00:05:07.232
It also explains why changing frequency in one medium often forces wavelength to adjust if the speed stays fixed.

63
00:05:07.232 --> 00:05:12.975
Keep this takeaway in view: Wave speed is controlled by the product, so different pairs can lead to the same travel speed.

64
00:05:12.975 --> 00:05:18.719
Pair one means 4 Hz and 3 m.

65
00:05:18.719 --> 00:05:24.463
The product gives a wave speed of 12 metres per second.

66
00:05:24.463 --> 00:05:30.206
Pair two means 6 Hz and 2 m.

67
00:05:30.206 --> 00:05:35.950
A different pair can still produce the same speed.

68
00:05:35.950 --> 00:05:39.582
Try four quick checks.

69
00:05:39.582 --> 00:05:43.214
What does frequency tell you?

70
00:05:43.214 --> 00:05:46.846
The number of cycles launched each second.

71
00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:50.478
What does wavelength tell you?

72
00:05:50.478 --> 00:05:54.110
The spacing between matching points.

73
00:05:54.110 --> 00:05:57.742
If frequency is four hertz and wavelength is three metres, what is the speed?

74
00:05:57.742 --> 00:06:01.374
Twelve metres per second.

75
00:06:01.374 --> 00:06:05.006
And if the wavelength is given in centimetres, what should you think about before substituting?

76
00:06:05.006 --> 00:06:08.638
Unit conversion.

77
00:06:08.638 --> 00:06:12.270
Those four checks capture the whole working structure of the lesson.

78
00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:15.902
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: the meanings, the formula, and the unit discipline are all in place.

79
00:06:15.902 --> 00:06:19.534
Frequency means launch rate.

80
00:06:19.534 --> 00:06:23.166
Count how many cycles appear each second.

81
00:06:23.166 --> 00:06:26.798
Wavelength means matching-point spacing.

82
00:06:26.798 --> 00:06:30.430
Measure crest to crest or compression to compression.

83
00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:35.367
You are ready for the worked example now.

84
00:06:35.367 --> 00:06:40.304
Start by identifying which two quantities are already known and which one the question wants.

85
00:06:40.304 --> 00:06:45.241
Say the meanings out loud if necessary.

86
00:06:45.241 --> 00:06:50.178
Frequency is launch rate.

87
00:06:50.178 --> 00:06:55.114
Wavelength is front spacing.

88
00:06:55.114 --> 00:07:00.051
Speed is disturbance travel rate.

89
00:07:00.051 --> 00:07:04.988
Next check the units, especially whether the wavelength is already in metres.

90
00:07:04.988 --> 00:07:09.925
Then apply v equals f times lambda, or rearrange carefully if the unknown is frequency or wavelength.

91
00:07:09.925 --> 00:07:14.862
Finally, ask whether the answer makes physical sense in the wave picture.

92
00:07:14.862 --> 00:07:19.799
That full method is stronger than substitution alone, and it is what this lesson is trying to build.

93
00:07:19.799 --> 00:07:24.736
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: frequency, wavelength, and speed now behave like one connected system.

94
00:07:24.736 --> 00:07:29.673
Step 1: Pick out the known and unknown wave quantities.

95
00:07:29.673 --> 00:07:34.609
Step 2: Convert lengths and check the expected speed unit.

96
00:07:34.609 --> 00:07:39.546
identify the known two quantities and the unknown one.

97
00:07:39.546 --> 00:07:44.483
check the meanings and the units before substituting.

98
00:07:44.483 --> 00:07:49.420
The best wave-equation answers keep the physical picture attached all the way through the arithmetic.
